<Layouts.app flash={@flash} current_user={assigns[:current_user]}>
  <article class="legal">
    <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
    <p class="legal-date">Last updated {@updated}</p>

    <p>
      These terms cover your use of Traveling Poet at poet.travel. The service is run by
      Udi Bauman as an individual. Using it means accepting them. Questions: <a
        class="link"
        href={"mailto:#{@contact}"}
      >{@contact}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What the service is</h2>
    <p>
      You are given an AI poet that travels virtually through real places and writes you
      a daily illustrated journal. The travel is imagined; the places are real. What the
      poet writes and draws is made by a language model from sources it consulted, and
      it can be mistaken, out of date or incomplete. Read it as a letter from a
      traveller, not as travel advice, and check anything you plan to act on: opening
      hours, prices, safety, visas and the rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      The service is an early beta. Features change, entries and drawings may be lost,
      and the whole thing may be paused or stopped. There is no uptime promise.
    </p>

    <h2>Your account</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in with Google or with Apple, one account per person, and keep it to yourself. You must be
      16 or older. You are responsible for what is done through your account.
    </p>

    <h2>What you write, and what your poet writes</h2>
    <p>
      Your journal is yours. You keep the rights to what you write and to what your poet
      writes for you. You give the service permission to store, process and display that
      content so it can run: to show you the journal, to make books and PDFs from it,
      and, if you make the journal public, to show it to anyone with the link and on the
      home page map.
    </p>
    <p>
      The service will not use your journal or chats to advertise to you, and will not
      sell them. Anonymous, aggregated counts, such as how many entries the fleet wrote,
      may be used to describe or improve the service.
    </p>

    <h2>Using it fairly</h2>
    <p>Do not use Traveling Poet to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>break the law, or produce content that is illegal where you are</li>
      <li>harass, profile or expose private individuals</li>
      <li>
        push the poet into work outside the journal: no mass scraping, no contacting
        third parties, no attempt to read another account's data or the poet's own
        credentials
      </li>
      <li>
        resell, automate or overwhelm the service, or work around its quotas and caps
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Accounts that do these things can be suspended or deleted, and the sandbox behind
      them shut down.
    </p>

    <h2>Credits and payment</h2>
    <p>
      Days of travel are paid for in credits. A day as a Wanderer costs one credit and a
      day as a Trip Scout costs five; chatting and drawings are included in the day. A
      composed book edition costs a few credits; PDFs cost none. Rates are shown in
      Settings and can change, with notice in the app.
    </p>
    <p>
      Credits are prepaid, have no cash value, and are not transferable. Payments are
      handled by Stripe. If something goes wrong on the service's side, such as a day
      charged but never written, the credit comes back automatically; if it does not,
      write and it will be put right. Refunds of unused credits are at the operator's
      discretion, and nothing here limits rights you have by law where you live.
    </p>

    <h2>Your poet's sandbox</h2>
    <p>
      Each poet runs in its own sandbox, and what you ask it in chat steers what it
      does. Ask it to travel and write. Do not use it as general-purpose compute, and do
      not try to make it reach systems or people beyond the journal.
    </p>

    <h2>Ending it</h2>
    <p>
      You can stop at any time, and you can delete everything yourself under Account in
      Settings, or by writing to <a class="link" href={"mailto:#{@contact}"}>{@contact}</a>
      from your account's email address. Deletion removes your account, poet, journal,
      drawings, chats, ledger and sandbox, and cannot be undone. Unused credits are not
      refunded on deletion unless the law says otherwise. The service may end an account
      that breaks these terms, or wind down with reasonable notice to account holders.
    </p>

    <h2>No warranty, and the limit of liability</h2>
    <p>
      The service is provided as it is, without warranties of any kind, to the extent
      the law allows. It is not liable for indirect or consequential loss, or for
      decisions taken on what a poet wrote. Where liability cannot be excluded, it is
      limited to what you paid in the twelve months before the claim.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      These terms can change. The date at the top changes with them, and account holders
      are told by email or in the app before a material change takes effect. Continuing
      to use the service after that means accepting the new terms.
    </p>

    <p class="legal-foot">
      <.link navigate={~p"/privacy"} class="link">Privacy Policy</.link>
      · <.link navigate={~p"/"} class="link">Home</.link>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layouts.app>
